## Running the Project

### Clone Repository

```
git clone <repository-url>
cd InventoryManagement
```

### Run without Docker

Run PostgreSQL locally and make sure the `DefaultConnection` in
`InventoryManagement.API/appsettings.Development.json` points to that database.
The default development launch profile uses `http://localhost:5050`; the HTTPS
profile also serves `https://localhost:7175`.

Apply migrations when setting up a fresh local database:

```powershell
dotnet ef database update --project InventoryManagement.Infrastructure --startup-project InventoryManagement.API
```

Start the backend API from the repository root:

```powershell
dotnet run --project InventoryManagement.API
```

Start the frontend from the `inventory-web` folder, not the repository root:

```powershell
cd inventory-web
npm install
npm run dev
```

For local frontend requests, set `VITE_API_BASE_URL` in
`inventory-web/.env.local` to the backend URL, for example:

```powershell
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5050
```

### Run with Docker

Set the JWT signing key outside source control before starting the container.
The application reads it from `Jwt__Key`. Keep `Jwt__Issuer`,
`Jwt__Audience`, and `Jwt__AccessTokenLifetimeMinutes` in configuration unless
you need to override them per environment.

Production Compose requires these environment variables:

```
POSTGRES_DB
POSTGRES_USER
POSTGRES_PASSWORD
VITE_API_BASE_URL
FRONTEND_HTTP_PORT
Database__ApplyMigrationsOnStartup
Jwt__Issuer
Jwt__Audience
Jwt__Key
Cors__AllowedOrigins__0
ReverseProxy__KnownNetworks__0
Bootstrap__Admin__Enabled
Bootstrap__Admin__UserName
Bootstrap__Admin__Email
Bootstrap__Admin__Password
```

The production compose file builds
`ConnectionStrings__DefaultConnection` from the PostgreSQL variables above.
Set `Authentication__AllowPublicRegistration=false` in Production unless you
intentionally want unauthenticated users to create accounts; the production
compose file sets it to `false`.
Set `Database__ApplyMigrationsOnStartup=false` in Production after the database
is initialized. Automatic migrations are disabled by default in Production; when
pending migrations exist and startup migrations are disabled, startup logs the
pending migration names and stops before identity bootstrapping.
Set `Bootstrap__Admin__Enabled=true` only when creating the first production
administrator. Remove or disable it after startup succeeds.
Set frontend CORS origins with indexed environment variables such as
`Cors__AllowedOrigins__0`, `Cors__AllowedOrigins__1`, and so on. Production
origins must be explicit HTTPS origins without paths, queries, fragments, or
wildcards. Local HTTP origins belong only in Development or Testing
configuration. JWT bearer authentication uses the `Authorization` header, so
CORS credentials are not enabled.
For reverse-proxy deployments, configure trusted forwarded-header sources with
`ReverseProxy__KnownProxies__0`, `ReverseProxy__KnownNetworks__0`, and
additional indexed entries as needed. Do not configure wildcard proxy trust.
Production Swagger is disabled by default; expose it only when you intentionally
want Swagger available in that environment.
Rate limits use fixed windows and can be overridden with the
`RateLimiting__...` environment variables listed above. Set only numeric
limits and window durations; do not use secrets in rate-limit configuration.
Production logging is configured separately in
`InventoryManagement.API/appsettings.Production.json`. Logs keep traceId
correlation, avoid request and response bodies, drop events that carry common
secret-bearing property names, and retain 30 daily rolling files by default.

Run database migrations as a controlled pre-deployment step:

```
dotnet ef database update --project InventoryManagement.Infrastructure --startup-project InventoryManagement.API
```

The current multi-company rollout assumes a fresh PostgreSQL database. Do not
apply these migrations as a live legacy-data conversion path for an existing
single-company installation. If an environment must be replaced, take a backup,
recreate or restore the database as a whole, then initialize the fresh schema
through registration or the configured bootstrap admin.

For a first Docker production deployment with a new named volume, you can set
`Database__ApplyMigrationsOnStartup=true` in the root `.env` for the first
successful start, then set it back to `false` and restart the stack.

Back up PostgreSQL with `pg_dump` before production deployments or maintenance.
See `docs/postgresql-backup-restore.md` for local and Docker backup commands,
restore procedure, integrity checks, and retention guidance.

```
docker compose up --build
```

The local compose file starts PostgreSQL, the API, and the frontend. PostgreSQL
runs on container port `5432` and is exposed to the host as `localhost:5433` to
avoid conflicts with a locally installed PostgreSQL server on `localhost:5432`.
The API connects to PostgreSQL inside the compose network with
`Host=postgres;Port=5432`.

For Production, use the production compose file instead of the local
Development compose file:

```
docker compose -f docker-compose.production.yml up -d --build
```

The production compose file runs PostgreSQL, the API, and the frontend as
separate containers. The API container keeps
`ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production`, stores logs in a persistent Docker volume,
connects to the `postgres` service, runs as the non-root .NET runtime user, and
checks `/health/ready`. PostgreSQL data is stored in the
`inventory_postgres_data` Docker volume. The frontend container builds the
React/Vite app and serves static files with nginx. The frontend is not merged
into the backend container.

Create a private `.env` beside `docker-compose.production.yml` before running
production compose:

```
cp production.env.example .env
```

Set `VITE_API_BASE_URL` to the public URL that browsers should use for the API.
Set `Cors__AllowedOrigins__0` to the public frontend origin. Do not commit real
secrets; the checked-in `production.env.example` contains placeholders only.
Set `POSTGRES_DB`, `POSTGRES_USER`, and `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` for the production
database container.

For multiple clients, use the same codebase with separate deployments:

* one private `.env` per client
* one separate PostgreSQL database or Docker volume per client
* the same API and frontend images built from this repository

If bind mounts are used instead of named volumes, make sure the mounted
PostgreSQL data and log directories are writable by their container users.

Production TLS is expected to terminate at a reverse proxy. Configure the
proxy to send `X-Forwarded-For` and `X-Forwarded-Proto`, and set
`ReverseProxy__KnownProxies__0` or `ReverseProxy__KnownNetworks__0` to trust
only that proxy or network. The app listens on HTTP port `8080` inside the
container and relies on forwarded headers for correct HTTPS behavior.

Swagger UI:

```
http://localhost:8080/swagger
```

Health checks:

```
http://localhost:8080/health/live
http://localhost:8080/health/ready
```

Use `/health/live` for process liveness and `/health/ready` for readiness
checks that require database connectivity. Health responses are intentionally
minimal and do not include connection strings or exception details.

The application automatically:

* Starts the API
* Persists PostgreSQL data and API logs using Docker volumes
* Applies pending EF Core migrations automatically only when configured, such as in Development

---

## Authentication Flow

```text
Register
  -> User account, default company, Owner membership, Free subscription
Login
  -> JWT access token and refresh token
Frontend bootstrap
  -> GET /api/Users/me
  -> Select active company
Company-scoped API requests
  -> Bearer token + X-Company-Id
Access token expires
  -> Refresh token rotation
  -> New access token
```

